uuid "c5482cde-9561-5647-92a3-a41687e26ae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨7,-3,4⟩+s⟨\\sqrt{5},-3,2⟩+t⟨-5,4,-2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨7,-3,4⟩+s⟨\\sqrt{5},-3,2⟩+t⟨-2,4,-5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260250} :answer-field/id #uuid "88d78a6d-4a26-5561-9d61-b00f371bc511" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262364}] :question/id #uuid "4a48e326-aaaf-5e46-9d89-fb7681e96383" :question/math-academy-id "q-128761" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $P(7,-3,4)$ and $Q(5,1,-1),$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨\\sqrt{5},-3,2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). We already have one vector $v=⟨\\sqrt{5},-3,2⟩.$ Let's also use the displacement vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨5,1,-1⟩-⟨7,-3,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,4,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $p=⟨7,-3,4⟩$ of the point $P,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+sv+t\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\ r & =⟨7,-3,4⟩+s⟨\\sqrt{5},-3,2⟩+t⟨-2,4,-5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "336bb496-d1df-5e9a-a07a-1ec951355ede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The plane is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302774} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8dad69-2acf-5472-9a3f-505a95ac3fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-2,-2,-2⟩+s⟨1,4,-14⟩+t⟨1,1,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303341} {:answer/id #uuid "b208b020-2e95-5012-90d1-880d0704ce1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,4,-14⟩+s⟨-2,-2,-2⟩+t⟨1,1,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303557} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a6125d-748b-50ac-93e6-06be995d0638" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨0,3,-19⟩+s⟨-2,-2,-2⟩+t⟨1,2,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303769} {:answer/id #uuid "feb8b07e-b16f-57b2-afdf-a8e6076cfea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,4,-14⟩+s⟨1,1,1⟩+t⟨1,-2,-26⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,4,-14⟩+s⟨1,1,1⟩+t⟨1,-2,-26⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304058} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a308e92-1035-59ed-9244-1f44706e0efe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304233}] :question/id #uuid "133e1de3-de48-5298-8948-a57726f1d155" :question/math-academy-id "q-33160" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $C(1,4,-14)$ and is parallel to the two distinct lines $r=⟨-1,5,7⟩+s⟨1,1,1⟩$ and $r=⟨0,3,-19⟩+t⟨-2,-2,-2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The direction vectors of the lines are ${v}_{1}=⟨-2,-2,-2⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨1,1,1⟩,$ respectively.\nBut ${v}_{2}=-2{v}_{1}.$ So, our lines are parallel. \n\nDespite this fact, two distinct parallel lines still determine a plane. Now: \n\n- from the equation of the first line, we get the point $P$ with the position vector $p=⟨-1,5,7⟩$ that lies on it, and\n\n- from the equation of the second line, we get the point $Q$ with the position vector $q=⟨0,3,-19⟩$ that lies on it.\n\nThe vector $u=q-p$ will be parallel to the required plane. \n\nComputing the coordinates of $u,$  we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨0,3,-19⟩-⟨-1,5,7⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-2,-26⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}∦{v}_{1},$ we can find the equation of our plane.\n\nWe know that the point $C(1,4,-14)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $c=⟨1,4,-14⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =c+s{v}_{1}+tu \\\\ r & =⟨1,4,-14⟩+s⟨1,1,1⟩+t⟨1,-2,-26⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338879 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49c5cfba-30b4-5cd2-bb7b-b9327c517321" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-1,-1,1⟩+s⟨2,5,-3⟩+t⟨0,-8,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336716} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa30567-9f99-5396-b1b9-f442d2fcaa29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-1,-1,1⟩+s⟨1,-7,-2⟩+t⟨0,-8,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336976} {:answer/id #uuid "81128713-0ba6-54d5-ad60-664ede84fab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-1,-1,1⟩+s⟨3,6,-4⟩+t⟨1,-7,-2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337202} {:answer/id #uuid "8afb07a6-2bd8-5edb-9ec6-22d1e7d02591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The plane is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337287} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e1912b-7b80-5650-8872-08ddd53cd44c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-1,-1,1⟩+s⟨3,6,-4⟩+t⟨2,5,-3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-1,-1,1⟩+s⟨3,6,-4⟩+t⟨1,-7,-2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337202} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c6c2ae9-bf70-5d0f-96e8-5aee0a9622bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338357}] :question/id #uuid "2d249e59-e724-5332-83de-2d46399e2dbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-71034" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $L(-1,-1,1),\\,M(2,5,-3)$, and $N(0,-8,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =⟨2,5,-3⟩-⟨-1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,6,-4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =⟨0,-8,-1⟩-⟨-1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-7,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $l=⟨-1,-1,1⟩$ of the point $l,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =l+s\\overset{\\to }{LM}+t\\overset{\\to }{LN} \\\\ r & =⟨-1,-1,1⟩+s⟨3,6,-4⟩+t⟨1,-7,-2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2337c867-1b68-51ab-bed8-d899af940e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The plane is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336359} {:answer/id #uuid "337484e0-5b6b-5f95-88dc-ef0b3096b39a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨2,-1,0⟩+s⟨3,4,-3⟩+t⟨2,0,0⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336518} {:answer/id #uuid "38e231b3-4c0e-5717-a0ef-ef6534f1c274" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨2,-1,0⟩+s⟨1,5,-3⟩+t⟨2,0,0⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336565} {:answer/id #uuid "4401b429-e065-55ec-a1ab-6464c74d2d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨2,-1,0⟩+s⟨1,5,-3⟩+t⟨0,1,0⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336670} {:answer/id #uuid "f760a7c7-7e6e-53d4-8222-5b484099a2f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨2,-1,0⟩+s⟨3,4,-3⟩+t⟨0,1,0⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨2,-1,0⟩+s⟨1,5,-3⟩+t⟨0,1,0⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336670} :answer-field/id #uuid "489447af-58f8-5125-a63d-1b42d95b0549" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338406}] :question/id #uuid "238f448c-c904-543b-b4aa-7c48d6e1e778" :question/math-academy-id "q-71038" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $U(2,-1,0),\\,V(3,4,-3)$, and $W(2,0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{UV}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{UW},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =⟨3,4,-3⟩-⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,5,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{UW} & =⟨2,0,0⟩-⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $u=⟨2,-1,0⟩$ of the point $U,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =u+s\\overset{\\to }{UV}+t\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\ r & =⟨2,-1,0⟩+s⟨1,5,-3⟩+t⟨0,1,0⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366205 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abe04ef1-0077-539a-b85c-52caa98344a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,1,1⟩+s⟨0,1,1⟩+t⟨-3,2,4⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366207} {:answer/id #uuid "4a96f288-3fd0-537b-806e-046844764150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The plane is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366208} {:answer/id #uuid "fac45cd4-59c7-516c-bd74-1e4a54e263c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,1,1⟩+s⟨-3,2,4⟩+t⟨1,1,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366209} {:answer/id #uuid "4e15b755-6aa5-59e8-8ede-166d794590c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,1,1⟩+s⟨0,1,1⟩+t⟨1,1,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366210} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4ca503-3a78-5aa5-ac98-b7e77b80a651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,1,1⟩+s⟨0,1,1⟩+t⟨-4,1,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,1,1⟩+s⟨0,1,1⟩+t⟨-4,1,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366211} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b9dde6c-05bb-5b0d-9060-015e400fc047" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366206}] :question/id #uuid "340957e8-d465-5c3d-b1df-5f238a4c6329" :question/math-academy-id "q-71040" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $C(1,1,1)$ and $D(-3,2,4)$, and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨0,1,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). We already have one vector $v=⟨0,1,1⟩.$ Let's also use the displacement vector $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =⟨-3,2,4⟩-⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $c=⟨1,1,1⟩$ of the point $C,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =c+sv+t\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\ r & =⟨1,1,1⟩+s⟨0,1,1⟩+t⟨-4,1,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5688ffe4-a344-59de-904d-69d4ee154888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,-1,-1⟩+t⟨3,5,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387959} {:answer/id #uuid "f1894930-5724-5ec7-9314-304ee3857bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,-2,3⟩+s⟨1,-2,-1⟩+t⟨5,3,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387960} {:answer/id #uuid "36f474e9-d65e-5772-a687-55109749a47b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,-1,-2⟩+t⟨3,5,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387961} {:answer/id #uuid "cb27fd48-3d2d-5982-947b-b3e559f28c8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,1,2⟩+t⟨-3,1,5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387962} {:answer/id #uuid "17405e35-9e5b-5d7e-a665-511b494914e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,-2,-1⟩+t⟨3,5,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,-2,-1⟩+t⟨3,5,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387963} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b619d06-46ec-54c2-b23b-cc9a1e50c422" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387958}] :question/id #uuid "d8ae8a52-91d4-5f03-bbaf-4e5a85108c69" :question/math-academy-id "q-128741" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $P(1,-2,3),\\,Q(3,-4,2),$ and $R(4,3,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨3,-4,2⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =⟨4,3,2⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,5,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $p=⟨1,-2,3⟩$ of the point $P,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+s\\overset{\\to }{PQ}+t\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\ r & =⟨1,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,-2,-1⟩+t⟨3,5,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60c5c0f5-cea3-5255-8903-d2285b902635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,2,-1⟩+s⟨0,-1,-1⟩+t⟨2,-1,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387966} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5a335a-8c39-5296-9221-80718d983791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,2,-1⟩+s⟨1,0,-1⟩+t⟨2,1,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387967} {:answer/id #uuid "a1feedd1-adb2-5c03-b2da-3a56801de734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,2,-1⟩+s⟨0,-1,0⟩+t⟨2,-3,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387968} {:answer/id #uuid "9a555223-84bf-5c63-a527-b176aedbb599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,2,-1⟩+s⟨1,0,-1⟩+t⟨-1,3,2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387969} {:answer/id #uuid "0604c24b-d46b-5997-bc46-46a6a514d076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,2,-1⟩+s⟨1,-1,1⟩+t⟨3,-1,2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,2,-1⟩+s⟨0,-1,-1⟩+t⟨2,-1,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387966} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0f3ed1e-aca6-5323-9130-d1a84b4303b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387965}] :question/id #uuid "0a7f0779-a02e-54b4-a490-a401988e7022" :question/math-academy-id "q-128745" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $D(3,2,-1),\\,E(3,1,-2),$ and $F(5,1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =⟨3,1,-2⟩-⟨3,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =⟨5,1,2⟩-⟨3,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $d=⟨3,2,-1⟩$ of the point $D,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =d+s\\overset{\\to }{DE}+t\\overset{\\to }{DF} \\\\ r & =⟨3,2,-1⟩+s⟨0,-1,-1⟩+t⟨2,-1,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11fe6c60-77f7-596d-b73e-0a2b1a46b507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,6,2⟩+s⟨-1,-15,-2⟩+t⟨2,-9,0⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393066} {:answer/id #uuid "18c27f61-10ac-5e9a-9262-ba682e6d266e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The plane is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393067} {:answer/id #uuid "a673c983-c8f1-5b15-8abb-116913587299" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,6,2⟩+s⟨-1,-15,-2⟩+t⟨0,-6,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393068} {:answer/id #uuid "f377602b-aa7b-52c3-8d62-a25cd478c050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,6,2⟩+s⟨0,-6,-1⟩+t⟨3,0,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393069} {:answer/id #uuid "37a16cbe-c7b9-5d98-99c1-895ad8adbfef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,6,2⟩+s⟨2,-9,0⟩+t⟨3,0,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,6,2⟩+s⟨-1,-15,-2⟩+t⟨0,-6,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393068} :answer-field/id #uuid "141932d6-a6f5-58b4-ba12-7fe47a40ab43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393065}] :question/id #uuid "901f1360-df93-5e5e-ac60-c5607de4f628" :question/math-academy-id "q-71036" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $P(3,6,2),\\,Q(2,-9,0)$, and $R(3,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨2,-9,0⟩-⟨3,6,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-15,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =⟨3,0,1⟩-⟨3,6,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-6,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $p=⟨3,6,2⟩$ of the point $P,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+s\\overset{\\to }{PQ}+t\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\ r & =⟨3,6,2⟩+s⟨-1,-15,-2⟩+t⟨0,-6,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f06646-216c-5c67-9be6-f9c0e54523f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,-2,0⟩+s⟨-3,-2,0⟩+t⟨1,0,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410867} {:answer/id #uuid "56c396c7-54c7-56b3-83a2-5e6b3f8f1725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,-2,0⟩+s⟨-1,5,-1⟩+t⟨4,2,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410871} {:answer/id #uuid "8e59d870-815b-50b7-be77-2034640ef7da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The plane is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410882} {:answer/id #uuid "918bb145-9b81-5373-9e09-80bc4d524835" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,-2,0⟩+s⟨-1,5,-1⟩+t⟨-3,-2,0⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410883} {:answer/id #uuid "c9066316-4e88-5b51-afd6-db0ca189a8ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,-2,0⟩+s⟨-1,5,-1⟩+t⟨1,0,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,-2,0⟩+s⟨-1,5,-1⟩+t⟨4,2,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410871} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffaa26fc-cae0-5499-800c-9dd489ee2f5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410897}] :question/id #uuid "59e1275b-72d1-5efa-8072-c2d963838f7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71049" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $U(-3,-2,0)$ and $V(1,0,-1)$, and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-1,5,-1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). We already have one vector $v=⟨-1,5,-1⟩.$ Let's also use the displacement vector $\\overset{\\to }{UV}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =⟨1,0,-1⟩-⟨-3,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $u=⟨-3,-2,0⟩$ of the point $U,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =u+sv+t\\overset{\\to }{UV} \\\\ r & =⟨-3,-2,0⟩+s⟨-1,5,-1⟩+t⟨4,2,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "076c0841-49e0-5efa-932b-cea12451f354" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+s⟨-2,-2,5⟩+t⟨-3,4,5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429935} {:answer/id #uuid "36f17219-1319-57b9-b736-3daca3fe9f64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+s⟨-2,-2,5⟩+t⟨-2,4,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429936} {:answer/id #uuid "42182090-ca90-5aff-919b-f97f28d17f45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+s⟨-3,-2,7⟩+t⟨-3,4,5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429937} {:answer/id #uuid "a639639c-9709-5ea4-b9d7-eed8e4f97ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The plane is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429938} {:answer/id #uuid "dd4f3967-0094-5670-b34b-c4d617495978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+s⟨-2,4,3⟩+t⟨-3,4,5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+s⟨-3,-2,7⟩+t⟨-3,4,5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429937} :answer-field/id #uuid "918bca49-2085-5b3c-9bbd-1ed60948277f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429940}] :question/id #uuid "e59ecbbf-3ac2-51fb-8da8-83763d787a9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71033" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $D(1,0,-2),\\,E(-2,-2,5)$, and $F(-2,4,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =⟨-2,-2,5⟩-⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-2,7⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =⟨-2,4,3⟩-⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,4,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $d=⟨1,0,-2⟩$ of the point $D,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =d+s\\overset{\\to }{DE}+t\\overset{\\to }{DF} \\\\ r & =⟨1,0,-2⟩+s⟨-3,-2,7⟩+t⟨-3,4,5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of the Plane Passing Through Three Given Points"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1806 :topic/title "The Parametric Equations of a Plane"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158396} :activity/id #uuid "97fd4430-aac7-55ae-afde-da3c6748fa89" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158396 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158469 :tutorial/content "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\n \n\nwe know that if $A$ has distinct eigenvalues, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$\n\nThis formula works even when the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1, \\lambda_2$ are complex. However, using it directly requires working with complex exponentials, which can be cumbersome.\n\nA more direct method is to find just *one* of the complex solutions, say $\\mathbf x_1(t) = \\mathbf{v}_1 e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_1} t},$ and separate it into its *real and imaginary parts*.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t} \\\\  & ={{{w}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Real Part}}\\,+\\,\\text{i}\\,\\,\\,{{{w}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Imaginary Part}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general *real-valued* solution is then a linear combination of these two parts:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x(t) = c_1 \\mathbf w_1(t) + c_2 \\mathbf w_2(t)\n$$\n\n**Note:** This method may seem surprising. Why can we construct the general solution using only *one* of the complex solutions, $\\mathbf x_1(t)?$\n\nThe reason is that the second solution, $\\mathbf x_2(t),$ doesn't provide any new information. For a real matrix $A,$ the complex eigenvalues and eigenvectors come in *conjugate pairs*, which means $\\mathbf x_2(t)$ is simply the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf x_1(t).$\n\nSince $\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf w_1 (t) - \\textrm{i} \\, \\mathbf w_2(t),$ it is also a linear combination of the real and imaginary parts of $\\mathbf x_1(t).$\n\nTo build our general solution, we just need two *linearly independent* real solutions. The key facts are the following:\n\n- For a system with complex eigenvalues, the real part $\\mathbf w_1(t)$ and the imaginary part $\\mathbf w_2(t)$ are *always* linearly independent.\n\n- Since they are two linearly independent solutions, they can form the basis for the general solution.\n\nSo, we can use them directly and bypass the need to work with $\\mathbf x_2(t)$ at all.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "a93ff6d6-1eb0-5e42-8b69-ae73fc891839" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4283 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "24b6ab49-7a81-5a3b-a140-aabc60a9f348" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158397}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158397 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e3dd935-2058-5b28-bdbb-0c45586730d9"} :step/id #uuid "33bad08d-6b45-5628-aba5-8acb1bac5825" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158398}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158398 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec2743cd-4e26-59d5-9368-7a77fa4d0527"} :step/id #uuid "9044a6cf-4cf6-55f0-81fe-5f88ad0b36be" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158399}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158399 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3692fa3-14c5-5db9-8fee-e99ae35e386f"} :step/id #uuid "671c8d98-22a1-54a0-98cc-259902e11176" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158400}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158400 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1633293-c176-5bfe-8fa9-cfd872ceca26"} :step/id #uuid "911f4d76-3073-51d2-8506-25330ab817ac"}] :activity/title "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs With Complex Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157477}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186050309 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157448 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157069 :question/id #uuid "2a284986-5300-5bad-a33c-aa2bc47712b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7043" :question/problem "![](images/23/2376d4518853bf4a71511133c709d5fdabadb3db94c88f941274dd7d9557dd5a.svg)\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n![](images/12/12ba5728836320b3d99d138507e5bb71e7f3a6bd2d93635443b7c26bb71a7ddd.svg)\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/fa/faa6212a47ec98f63248d7de696decaaba0b767e5771eef523ec1e9373051f21.svg)*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/d3/d33b2b3ad04f460f27c6f199b9f54ae327f48a96d1b3de8ce270e677440cac74.svg) \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/9d/9d5190da8a9a59e2691c8ab16e366be20a14c6c7ce5b8f57986d4faa50cceb9e.svg)\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1$ is \n$\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n![](images/61/61c5ce70ef3272667953b10eb895ba8c480723399d5aeb96eae0e0f54064c1e1.svg)\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t) = \\cos{t} + \\sin{t}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e3dd935-2058-5b28-bdbb-0c45586730d9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157741 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39a5dad7-c94f-51da-a677-ef81f5bf32cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302815} {:answer/id #uuid "3ac8dcd5-8feb-5327-aa14-8382a8c89d1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302822} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec9adaa-1031-5be5-9bbc-b931868084e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302950} {:answer/id #uuid "522ee2e1-5f6a-5437-9867-bb0bb77e89b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302966} {:answer/id #uuid "753eaf97-31e8-58ae-bd6a-f193b0665738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302822} :answer-field/id #uuid "33ba3425-036d-579b-bf3d-66b6d684a1b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304213}] :question/id #uuid "284bef67-027d-5606-82aa-6c0a65dd3c2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-328592" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157748 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79870c04-57ca-5781-a92d-d12022741fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303206} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ca72aab-192c-5a09-b47f-f93c5e3c2491" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304149} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ee20c11-844d-5c24-a1b3-2a84b2d0831f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303344} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0d7f793-c358-5ed5-a56b-c36c50c622d9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304690}] :question/id #uuid "f2ec92c3-9f21-5a42-ab9d-d5296a6ec794" :question/math-academy-id "q-329578" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}g(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}.$*\n\n$f(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,$\n\n$g(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nf(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,g(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "146c3842-e572-58de-a189-f4968ebcf1db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305326} {:answer/id #uuid "333ca2e0-544d-53bd-9158-282fa5561eea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305634} {:answer/id #uuid "63043a6c-acc5-5aa3-b855-e4c4bbff7b7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306110} {:answer/id #uuid "87d08246-6706-50ee-9558-ecaf2832df76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306445} {:answer/id #uuid "cdfb662f-e3e5-5390-b5b6-af0ebce58084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307056} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c2ea217-4efd-561d-8b07-d98a5840f553" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307913}] :question/id #uuid "ca67e8b6-0234-571f-a67b-fda3fdb0ac5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333908" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ef07c57-7b26-532e-abce-c3b4260f341a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305761} {:answer/id #uuid "a38ab478-eb09-55a3-b7f8-86078f31b2e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306693} {:answer/id #uuid "e22b2984-cfc4-520b-aefb-e79c842df8ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307246} {:answer/id #uuid "ec27d123-54dd-536e-ab63-b9b60e28a6d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307325} {:answer/id #uuid "fcbac6c6-b5e1-5e28-a463-3a6033872351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307246} :answer-field/id #uuid "2226386f-9a4a-51e3-9eba-3e5d30e157e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307631}] :question/id #uuid "5689a8d6-4ee4-559f-9467-3ae542252695" :question/math-academy-id "q-333909" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c195f85-68ea-58c6-883c-02aa2e44f3a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}(12t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}(12t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305880} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b997a90-0a34-5bd6-bb26-14eec54e8e0f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307615} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02fcdf82-e4a6-5769-ac92-d8f9c397f052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}(12t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}(12t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305181} :answer-field/id #uuid "5017b989-af63-5cdf-87a1-f9f7c1dca79e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307766}] :question/id #uuid "23c54f10-fdd2-5cf5-a512-18e39d6dc69a" :question/math-academy-id "q-333916" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 18 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}f(t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}g(t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i}.$*\n\n$f(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,g(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 18 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-12\\text{i} & 18 \\\\ -8 & -12\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\text{12i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\text{12i}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)-3\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nf(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t),\\,g(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1995fd1a-c6d4-512a-a6be-f138f303bc89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414106} {:answer/id #uuid "b06b432f-2968-54b1-befd-eb3d08349e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414118} {:answer/id #uuid "b573e05d-11ff-53a0-89be-3bf5e5e75031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414119} {:answer/id #uuid "e4941795-a7fd-5063-b84f-1b777cfccaeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414127} {:answer/id #uuid "fad372c2-2511-539e-bd31-2b0cf16c6b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414118} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc7055a9-5fc1-5c31-8fe0-833c83e126c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414134}] :question/id #uuid "ee5bc47e-f5eb-5ac3-a599-35150942e1b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-333911" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}f(t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157449 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157070 :question/id #uuid "8e797f4b-fe53-5d0a-a4b0-8fb51ee04c92" :question/math-academy-id "e-7044" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n![](images/0c/0c7d4c9f4782665ddfa9e36fa52957f308e49d6e42e75f94bb01fc998bbce219.svg)\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/ae/ae46b58d986d4c703470adb45128c046e6e0e572ce92593a0661e990bcb1b661.svg)*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/ea/eaa7fa7c58b91792b295e853a01912a5ec45edda30f5de68f5e3952a73fae014.svg)\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/a2/a290109e5eb086926e15a1cffa9e11dd54b8843b3891068af7c322d10baa20de.svg)\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1$ is \n$\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n![](images/1e/1ec89bb86150c8b5002835d1f7a8f796f514e8c05f70303c833d13e922d5d390.svg)\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition ![](images/b2/b263aa33a22f008a8cc114136e985b5655cd0f097584c48311bb28eca256c2d2.svg) Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =1\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec2743cd-4e26-59d5-9368-7a77fa4d0527" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157742 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81c3ce5b-8f9a-5624-892f-59ef28bed87e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t - \\sin t \\\\ -\\cos t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155291} {:answer/id #uuid "86a381b3-701f-58b6-8fa5-25d4b399250f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}3\\sin t + \\cos t \\\\ \\sin t - \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155292} {:answer/id #uuid "27678edb-ac66-5f48-a8bb-ee080c4f87cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}3\\sin t + \\cos t \\\\ 2\\sin t - \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155293} {:answer/id #uuid "3b2670b4-1b0c-569f-b5b9-d567ed06b42f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sin t + \\cos t \\\\ \\sin t - \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155294} {:answer/id #uuid "7876586a-5496-5f41-a634-f2f77c3a6696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t \\\\ -\\cos t + \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155295}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0db37bc8-1bcd-5713-bf25-df44edfdddcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155290} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4777c8cc-32f9-5990-a586-542488fe0c9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390806} {:answer/id #uuid "0a85b2a0-6bd9-5830-a4eb-8625750ef041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390807} {:answer/id #uuid "1e8eb4b2-b64f-5669-b200-663db79825b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390808} {:answer/id #uuid "05e4a4ea-7b03-5e95-8cd2-8a040841abd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390809} {:answer/id #uuid "a449c519-39d9-5440-a74f-baf2eaca3c54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390809} :answer-field/id #uuid "56d8e4b6-3562-55f9-a0a0-89ceb9085ef4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390805}] :question/id #uuid "1d223592-2920-513b-a14a-16d21224ccf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-328593" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 1 & -\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5792beb-68b7-5752-9299-9931b201d9c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (4t) - \\sin (4t) \\\\ -2\\cos (4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155297} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ed4a4e-c6aa-5a29-b251-9c8437a8fde3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (4t) - \\sin (4t) \\\\ \\sin (4t) - 2\\cos (4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155298} {:answer/id #uuid "fe42c99c-f5da-5d71-ae3d-3d3b663d5c39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (4t) + 3\\sin (4t) \\\\ 4\\sin (4t) - 2\\cos (4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155299} {:answer/id #uuid "046bebf3-0221-5a86-b5cb-9fa226499a8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (4t) \\\\ -3\\sin (4t) - 2\\cos (4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155300} {:answer/id #uuid "1d51f51b-b3db-529a-91f8-338b0b349048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (4t) + \\sin (4t) \\\\ \\sin (4t) - 2\\cos (4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155301}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ca50a474-910f-5d5f-bbff-61842cb9d3e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155296}] :question/id #uuid "251baddb-117b-548e-a5ac-85189fcaf8e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-328594" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t), x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue $λ_{1} = 4\\textrm{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector $v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02152eb7-37ef-56ba-8d18-88281476b4a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305170} {:answer/id #uuid "8f5365f8-577e-53c1-900d-5882f73a1ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306511} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed4f59d-0bb5-542e-bd1b-480fc6b542ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306641} {:answer/id #uuid "b778d48d-5f86-5c73-bb6d-b2020f607662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306864} {:answer/id #uuid "cffc727f-c1ca-51d2-992a-99806785bfb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305170} :answer-field/id #uuid "370c90aa-4734-5526-bc6d-5f546fd79364" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307697}] :question/id #uuid "ec6ae686-d666-58a4-bc66-27db653b0dac" :question/math-academy-id "q-334054" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =3\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308546 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14e5cb1f-a44e-5105-994e-13ee54016ce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305331} {:answer/id #uuid "32910bfc-ca9f-549a-8372-97b7e319e1d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305627} {:answer/id #uuid "481407f1-b6b8-5788-9b62-cd936266bfd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305851} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c935df-213f-54c9-850f-d8860501de20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306730} {:answer/id #uuid "dcbf58ef-36c3-5a31-8a44-d7a158d209d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305851} :answer-field/id #uuid "24745f64-82c5-5ea8-862e-caa9545b9064" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307635}] :question/id #uuid "6645eb63-a516-5aee-a18a-9a569b6c6f72" :question/math-academy-id "q-334059" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 8 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 8 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f0bc03-23e9-5844-a152-810bdf973b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{s