egin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 6 \\\\ -14 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480634 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "477ace4c-82e3-57c5-a4c3-6fc1dcc1600b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480228} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa983bc-fb00-52bc-b27d-c56b5262d607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}13 & -10 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480270} {:answer/id #uuid "6888f16e-6a85-521f-975e-63342031a0b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480281} {:answer/id #uuid "c9be896a-c20c-55d4-9452-71c1e8bfd917" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480446} {:answer/id #uuid "e57c7b8f-280a-5e63-a865-80fca1154fb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480228} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bb5b3db-09a2-5068-870b-5e729655be69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480554}] :question/id #uuid "090d811d-2518-5f77-a8e6-7fb33e3ee0bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-97113" :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ -14 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis Using a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129131 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128942 :question/id #uuid "4b287ac7-7569-59bc-a319-0e62996083bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5517" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6dea9ab921904f09ef76230fe87194b745f4f06eb9cfbbbbda0b3bd44722fc93.svg)\n\nConsider the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ and standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{S}} = P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is\n\n![](images/01/01d05455345f8ec71e58f613d3bcf54c41f4ff196766c728871e54050f590fba.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 3-(-7)\\cdot (-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 13 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "969220db-a686-511b-b33a-0b4f6ab66b1a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5669535f-e936-5808-865c-2ffa3ed1afbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324692} {:answer/id #uuid "686be3d1-f70d-534b-8c1d-122b77f7f1cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324843} {:answer/id #uuid "9c139bdc-ae5e-5940-826c-9936fe328b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325293} {:answer/id #uuid "c6078509-e79b-537e-83f5-08de93f7a92f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325680} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c8c242-befa-55ad-8739-21acb222c6b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325293} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8500d2d-7918-5e80-9683-a3d7c1f7ecbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326549}] :question/id #uuid "3ef316a2-414a-55cd-b85b-e6597918be4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-38362" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129642 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a40aa1ac-5263-5150-bf9d-6f4a0ec37bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 13\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127334} {:answer/id #uuid "d