e "\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388732} {:answer/id #uuid "e40a8c23-f4bf-5ce3-83ac-967fca612333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 7 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388733} {:answer/id #uuid "e15417fe-4e3a-5dc9-85cb-29bd49278518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 10 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388734} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f1420d-53ef-5213-998f-f80664faf30f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388735} {:answer/id #uuid "d01304c8-d666-5269-97b9-3cec349e53cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ -9 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388735} :answer-field/id #uuid "862e16c6-55ff-5a1b-beec-90fa0f9883e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388731}] :question/id #uuid "a4d35477-b857-55bc-8644-6d12a78d9e7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140481" :question/problem "$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -8 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66801734-cfb2-5c71-a490-c1b7e646ceaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392200} {:answer/id #uuid "f53c3f35-1d53-5e23-b182-ed548d11c6e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392201} {:answer/id #uuid "410cee51-9e9d-5f87-9f7c-d4d7a404f090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ -8 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392202} {:answer/id #uuid "8113ca7b-5184-5b33-8a9d-b6e8ed5f40b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 8 \\\\ -10 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392203} {:answer/id #uuid "ef6cbce4-288c-5941-8ac1-8ff4f6a5a54f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 11 \\\\ -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 8 \\\\ -10 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392203} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fbf9e10-b95b-5f24-bb1a-b9ddc49a6ea2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392199}] :question/id #uuid "f01f26b9-71a9-52a6-8f28-546cd5a00ed8" :question/math-academy-id "q-38455" :question/problem "$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 4-3\\cdot (-5)}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 8 \\\\ -10 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "245cb731-469d-572a-8764-194ea7fe088a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395245} {:answer/id #uuid "f54a6b7e-61b3-5ff8-b9a8-9f20f110085a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -8 \\\\ -16 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395246} {:answer/id #uuid "398a11ff-8023-5bd5-89f6-9f7aad0aa834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -4 \\\\ -8 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395247} {:answer/id #uuid "59ef0fc5-8743-5c5e-a125-6c5746e60c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395248} {:answer/id #uuid "4e5b8992-a502-5704-b901-de8d8c4d35b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-13 & -4 \\\\ -7 & -11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395245} :answer-field/id #uuid "26a8ce4a-4ead-5a11-85a0-c90e44fcc7e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395244}] :question/id #uuid "3a316dac-2644-5b90-b954-acca3323828e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97121" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -4 \\\\ -8 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b6934d8-eaf0-5f13-b06a-71ac2a0dc0af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419660} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9c3643-ad71-51e0-bd19-40d272c2cabe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419667} {:answer/id #uuid "8f47a664-a9ad-5deb-90c8-17a7a38adab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 7 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419671} {:answer/id #uuid "d0704113-7386-54ca-ad0c-7578acd9ac62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -11 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419676} {:answer/id #uuid "dc81c6b4-cc09-56a5-916c-e323cc4197ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419667} :answer-field/id #uuid "32c807d8-09ff-597f-a633-55d26150ec43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419681}] :question/id #uuid "662e0d41-a6b8-58b3-ad4a-e835c48f17e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-97118" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480673 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25d0c1b4-61e7-5e25-9833-9ff8521f8e61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -11 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480171} {:answer/id #uuid "a84373ce-6910-52c1-988d-e8fbee200996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480395} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a541f3-2fce-5116-8f9a-bbdea23325fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 15 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480409} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c07fb2-8a16-5c6a-8258-d32151d0964c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480440} {:answer/id #uuid "f7563451-68a5-5c50-b602-20f48ffbfffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -11 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480171} :answer-field/id #uuid "86fb28cc-fb24-5ba3-a4c6-598034db31b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480578}] :question/id #uuid "79dda897-ef6c-5dc8-918f-90eef254a83f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97132" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 7}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 41 \\\\ 5 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -11 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to the Standard Basis"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129155 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128973 :question/id #uuid "5e4b6303-8ec5-5ba6-ba1d-3b28ff8924b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5897" :question/problem "![](images/4e/4ef9218607e5b3285d98967ec7cf44627ece6ecc5918f702fded329b1fe8ee4d.svg)\n\nConsider the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$, shown above. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n![](images/26/26210267b80b50c4296225ac67237767cb326f2863fbfeba4cbdeb3f256ef62e.svg)\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n![](images/97/97450c835af63c4489b848a69986d5b496af1bf983355c65021aca3384826ca7.svg)\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -9 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "86028fae-a119-5194-975d-a6d9fad6faf5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34b8c4b6-b53f-5fd6-8a59-c4c18583dd9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-25 & -14 \\\\ 16 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392233} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa442b6-3b37-5157-88fd-f23ac8a4d42a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -18 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392234} {:answer/id #uuid "700fe7c1-5ebe-5a72-b683-b35432a45b97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392235} {:answer/id #uuid "7362374a-a152-5b2b-adfe-f73b7788011b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -10 \\\\ -4 & 13\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392236} {:answer/id #uuid "991e6247-97fb-5467-ad97-075e201709bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 6 \\\\ -20 & -11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -18 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392234} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ffe2e2a-b586-5e41-933b-844875782379" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392232}] :question/id #uuid "ef695398-bdfe-5a30-85b6-97e0a8733294" :question/math-academy-id "q-38761" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 11 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -5 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 11 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 11 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 7-2\\cdot 4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -18 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1079d3d2-be2f-5cb3-b94c-9018f0762790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -38 \\\\ 8 & -19\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480143} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab2b0d1-3d5f-53f9-8f37-02b8cfded0cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ -8 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480239} {:answer/id #uuid "53b397b9-c829-5c9b-b0ea-a550e68d9a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -8 \\\\ 38 & -15\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480252} {:answer/id #uuid "702184cc-df91-5774-bfc1-66a9113e145d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & 19 \\\\ 38 & 57\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480290} {:answer/id #uuid "7146fe46-6a51-56c2-a398-39cdace60f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-13 & 9 \\\\ -16 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-13 & 9 \\\\ -16 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480294} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd51970d-d30d-5586-b9c7-74ad40f631a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127687}] :question/id #uuid "ba0e0a4a-0049-5942-9e25-8da63ac0f245" :question/math-academy-id "q-38804" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)\\cdot 7-5\\cdot (-4)}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-13 & 9 \\\\ -16 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05aad4b2-f972-578e-af5a-26dfc8a438af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}13 & -11 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269319} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad7c5fe-40f8-522f-bdbe-c53bd6fe6af8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 13 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270077} {:answer/id #uuid "85d25069-b2ef-500f-b5a0-82957e48e706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -13 \\\\ 11 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270428} {:answer/id #uuid "8f25d38d-9256-5bce-93bb-0ee574b9e627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 \\\\ 13 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270514} {:answer/id #uuid "ade88c7f-6c40-5977-b84b-478753c47604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -13 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -13 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270818} :answer-field/id #uuid "66aa8b4c-adfa-5e24-b81e-708e43c4a82c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271692}] :question/id #uuid "29e136e7-2017-5bc6-b4f3-fbb402d6dab1" :question/math-academy-id "q-140490" :question/problem "$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}},\\,C={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -11 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -13 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326717 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b56c7fa-316c-5445-a9f9-556b16279803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324314} {:answer/id #uuid "65c34457-4423-5308-a834-d913d6ba29b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324815} {:answer/id #uuid "7d693edf-cdc6-56ec-95dd-f9c25e7d7363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325028} {:answer/id #uuid "819a882b-cb9f-5cbb-bc9c-c62bf2ac9846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325057} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6bcd92-a28b-5a07-82f5-aeda4f84057f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ -22 & -15\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325028} :answer-field/id #uuid "239bff4c-ca29-5f80-9ffc-ce59e4ddf91b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326281}] :question/id #uuid "250a5e42-a5b7-575f-bc45-da1c84a71fbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-38809" :question/problem "$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}},\\,C={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360776 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1acbfcf4-eef4-5e10-9293-955304ed4643" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357982} {:answer/id #uuid "2518ebb0-c04b-5a83-9b14-2a22b6592f4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -12 \\\\ -10 & 18\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358083} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9c0121-9fc8-5689-9ab5-b5f35ce55187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358138} {:answer/id #uuid "3288b5fc-9467-576f-90f6-b17447b3185d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358202} {:answer/id #uuid "eac08a40-1ec6-5f86-8789-bd6a699a855d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357982} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6b18c30-7bb0-5953-baef-6b75d504f744" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360315}] :question/id #uuid "9c1ef303-e889-5fc1-8e48-5dba05ad5f94" :question/math-academy-id "q-97278" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360777 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0250b576-7f5a-5cdb-8d5f-4ac4eb5eea0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 9 \\\\ 8 & 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357778} {:answer/id #uuid "5d643694-922c-500b-9562-ceda51bb281d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -7 \\\\ 8 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358565} {:answer/id #uuid "bcab7151-1545-5adb-9acc-5a85e3563222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359394} {:answer/id #uuid "cf01b12a-1505-5270-a98f-d1009d985cb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359571} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c09573-bff3-5408-8c4e-15427f964077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359394} :answer-field/id #uuid "4184f1b0-a361-5e25-b40e-1d65214674e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360088}] :question/id #uuid "af8cde90-b41c-51e7-9e5a-28cfa06594f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-97282" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 8 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -7 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360778 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "515e0fc9-c112-5e74-b91b-3ea3532dd376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 7 \\\\ -7 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358451} {:answer/id #uuid "a2fc8074-c516-5c72-9d38-fc34219514b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -7 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359172} {:answer/id #uuid "d1364a16-2c06-53ff-a9a6-e2611b80cc11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359582} {:answer/id #uuid "e0062b56-f425-5478-b8b5-1d4bcea7c1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 \\\\ -8 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359717} {:answer/id #uuid "efb0e7ec-bdc2-5237-b2c4-a06fbcfdfda5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359888} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f6b3143-bd7d-5d87-90bd-f6e69c8080aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360207}] :question/id #uuid "b8a45af8-9088-5825-b97b-d38a2bf5a12a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97283" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 10 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 10 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -14 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 10 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -7 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 3-(-7)\\cdot (-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 7 \\\\ 21 & -15\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f79f3f12-b655-5b64-a7bf-a517e67f0b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 11 \\\\ -9 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375118} {:answer/id #uuid "043754e3-c713-58db-b2fc-5167e1133e99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375119} {:answer/id #uuid "3c7ce013-3b9e-5223-81e3-0d263fff5950" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -8 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375120} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e367f1-d29a-5954-8707-737b675f2f64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375121} {:answer/id #uuid "50221909-0120-5623-92a7-32d45ac0ff63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375121} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b21a007-3616-5457-8387-93e955f89993" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375117}] :question/id #uuid "17656f6a-e2ee-5663-8d6e-0b2fc48e129a" :question/math-academy-id "q-38808" :question/problem "$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}},\\,C={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -10 & 13 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 9 & -11\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -10 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -21 & 28\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{7}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -10 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-4)-(-3)\\cdot 5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dba848e-8a50-579f-aa1a-cc8b6a92a123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395258} {:answer/id #uuid "8afbc7d4-59ea-5795-91f6-cca9cb7bb68f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/