_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 8 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 8 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -11 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot (-11)-4\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 & -6 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f012443-8bd4-52ba-9493-3919a35ddba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395265} {:answer/id #uuid "47fce7e4-6451-5159-ab17-02eca79cf3cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395266} {:answer/id #uuid "77558324-5aee-52a7-9c40-5a51f9aaca4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395267} {:answer/id #uuid "9411351f-be5d-5884-a210-6d5c30bf0df5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395268} {:answer/id #uuid "3e925a83-495e-56e8-8d9b-c8d313746fdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395268} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2c44b4f-f29e-547e-a303-1a31ffccb3ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395264}] :question/id #uuid "90dac6d1-6fdb-5236-89cf-8823a20cc32a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97281" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "094350f2-e017-53e0-b0e9-5075268dbe4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419659} {:answer/id #uuid "248c47da-3dc5-5333-809d-acf62be00f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -12 \\\\ 7 & 14\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419663} {:answer/id #uuid "7d527527-44f7-5758-b7fd-4c2cd30a0912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419670} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cdd80b-8123-585f-9ada-143e7f38d137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419674} {:answer/id #uuid "cd75b99f-ca84-5149-9b32-732c0d712c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & -18 \\\\ -9 & -16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -12 \\\\ 7 & 14\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419663} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c3a3177-a079-5cdb-a624-7225c5f182a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419680}] :question/id #uuid "7afd5a54-c223-5e70-9582-9406545b2d06" :question/math-academy-id "q-97279" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -12 \\\\ 7 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d7f229-8e52-50a6-aefb-b0cac4224645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -11 \\\\ 5 & -10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480148} {:answer/id #uuid "22b7fe3f-06c5-50bf-974c-0d0810764de8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -11 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480169} {:answer/id #uuid "2861eb9c-4514-5616-ba03-e8561c891eed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -5 \\\\ 11 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480177} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcfa639-1ecc-55b1-9be1-e5cb4a37903f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -10 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480243} {:answer/id #uuid "50c0d953-a401-5471-947d-1ca57c74ec8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 \\\\ 10 & -11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -11 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480169} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8dc1988-bf2a-5231-bb4f-7e3b1ddd7888" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480626}] :question/id #uuid "d84dee64-5460-51d7-b92e-0ebbf9e09851" :question/math-academy-id "q-140486" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[T]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot (-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 & -9 \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -11 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Given Basis"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129213 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129032 :question/id #uuid "c269a1d0-a47c-5f5c-96d8-4da2ecd35a48" :question/math-academy-id "e-8563" :question/problem "Let $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C},$ respectively. If $P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $\\mathcal{C}$ to basis $\\mathcal{B},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}}\\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1}$\n\nII. $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$\n\nIII. $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{B} \\to \\mathcal{C}}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/2e/2e82c232614bec8a98f079ea56d7913ee13e7b457e33e418198278d2e369fc5c.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a7c2b7-b4a3-5168-84dc-aab27f106193" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448614} {:answer/id #uuid "2902f1ff-cac7-5d97-a5a9-24543413f25f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448615} :answer-field/id #uuid "918a8f88-e411-59fb-b196-0dadf7f4fcbc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449347} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "533a5b8f-639e-5a45-8484-cb33ae7d5e46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448616} {:answer/id #uuid "e69f24f0-07c6-5983-ac28-bd000fd09e27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448616} :answer-field/id #uuid "54d6a6ae-1fa2-556d-9175-9c73166225ee" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449348} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2c7db1c-97d8-53db-84ae-5422607ceccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448618} {:answer/id #uuid "3c254231-3e39-568b-8359-80c766da4887" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448619} :answer-field/id #uuid "db2048e9-209c-59b8-a528-935ef72da977" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449349}] :question/id #uuid "4a7452af-fe2e-5944-8a03-a396c12645a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-38360" :question/problem "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $B$ to basis $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$\n- **II.** $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- **III.** $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129656 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9846df58-2b86-560c-b331-a6167b720b69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127418} {:answer/id #uuid "9a644ae9-0848-5c1e-ad82-84c279d1a889" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127419} {:answer/id #uuid "2799b11c-f355-50ef-85cc-7052bbbd97b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127420} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb948de-7f73-5c2b-a803-1e4e155d24d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127421} {:answer/id #uuid "7d1f5cbb-c1fd